WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.640
SPEAKER: Over the
past decade, we've

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.380
seen a drastic change in the
types of traffic patterns

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.020
that application developers
have to plan for.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.020
One day, you could be at
standard traffic, and the next,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.890
you need to scale to handle
tens of thousands more visitors.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.379
This, of course, leads
to all sorts of issues

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:20.050
with cost, response
times, and scale,

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.780
and there's no place
in your application

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.940
where this is felt more
than in your database.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.670
Whether it's dealing
with scale or just

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.700
struggling to fit unstructured
data into a relational schema

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.220
design, most
relational databases

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.900
struggle to solve these issues.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.380
This is where
Couchbase comes in.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.850
As a highly scalable,
high-performance,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.760
NoSQL document
database, Couchbase

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.970
is one of the fastest
NoSQL databases available,

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.210
entirely designed with
modern applications in mind.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.880
And right now, there's nothing
more modern than running

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.250
a database using Kubernetes.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.210
See, Kubernetes has rapidly
increased in sophistication

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.060
with things like operators,
persistent volumes,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.000
helping developers transition
to the cloud and scale where

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.260
they need to.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.270
This is why we've built
a special Kubernetes

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.680
operator called the Couchbase
Autonomous Operator.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.840
And it gives you the ability
to deploy multi-node clusters

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.470
by writing a small configuration
and pushing it to Kubernetes,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.860
recovering automatically
from node failures,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.940
automatically scaling your
cluster up or down as load

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.340
from the application
changes ensuring

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.420
that nodes in the cluster
are evenly split across racks

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.280
or zones in the data center.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.670
You can also automatically
upgrade the cluster

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.340
to a newer version
of Couchbase Server

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.430
and manage cluster
configuration to ensure

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.340
that the configuration is
properly set in your cluster.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.260
And to help bring this power to
you, without all the headaches,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.780
we've teamed up with
the GCP Marketplace

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.902
to make deploying and managing
Couchbase completely automated.

00:01:55.902 --> 00:01:57.360
Let's take a quick
look at how easy

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.720
it is to set up a
Couchbase cluster on GKE

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.540
using the Couchbase Autonomous
Operator through the GCP

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.340
Marketplace.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.310
The first thing you need
to do is log into GCP,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.889
then navigate to the
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:14.160
Then click the Applications
link to get to the Applications

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.030
Marketplace.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.580
Click Deploy from the
Marketplace, and then

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.790
scroll down to get to the
Couchbase Autonomous Operator.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.990
Click the Configure button
to configure a new operator,

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.920
and then make sure that
the operator is going

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.950
to be installed into the
correct cluster, in our case,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.980
standard-cluster-1,
and click Deploy.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.680
It'll take a few moments to
actually deploy the operator,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.140
but once it's done, you
should get to a screen that

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.540
looks like this.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.040
This means that the Couchbase
operator has been successfully

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.832
deployed, and that you
can now move forward

00:02:42.832 --> 00:02:45.323
to creating your first
Couchbase cluster.

00:02:45.323 --> 00:02:46.740
The next thing
that you need to do

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.650
is define a configuration for
your first Couchbase cluster

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.760
that you want to create.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.100
In this example, we create
a three-node cluster

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.290
with the data, index,
and query service,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.290
one bucket named Default,
and we're running a Couchbase

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.300
Enterprise 5.5.2.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.690
Push this configuration to
your Kubernetes cluster,

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.230
and the operator
will automatically

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.090
start creating a new
Couchbase cluster

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.790
based on the specification
that we just defined.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.445
After a few minutes, you should
see that your Couchbase cluster

00:03:11.445 --> 00:03:14.070
is created, and you have access
to the Couchbase Administration

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.510
console.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.140
If you click on
the Servers button,

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.810
you'll see that we have a
three-node cluster created,

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.520
and if you click
on the Buckets tab,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.210
you'll see that we
have a default bucket.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:25.980
Just by running a
few commands, we

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.430
can use the Couchbase Operator
to deploy the cluster for us.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.362
If any of our
Couchbase nodes fail,

00:03:31.362 --> 00:03:33.570
then the operator will get
the Couchbase cluster back

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.423
into a healthy state.

00:03:35.423 --> 00:03:37.590
If we need to upgrade the
cluster to a later version

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.870
in the future, then we
can have the Operator take

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.940
care of the upgrade for us.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.640
We aim to make management of
Couchbase clusters a thing

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.850
of the past and envision a
world where users no longer need

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.190
to focus on managing
their infrastructure

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.217
and can spend all their time
building their applications.

00:03:53.217 --> 00:03:55.800
And when it comes to running the
Couchbase Autonomous Operator

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.890
on Google's GKE, it just works.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.350
Visit the GCP Marketplace
today to set up

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:06.170
a Couchbase cluster on GKE,
or visit the link below

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:06.800
to learn more.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

